1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,360
We're going to go over the MIPS R10K and implementation of the MIPS 4 ISA.

2
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:17,200
In the last lecture, we introduced the scoreboard implementation for out-of-order execution as

3
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,600
well as an intro on register renaming and speculation.

4
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:26,640
Now we'll be describing the MIPS R10K implementation, which handles false dependencies as well

5
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:33,160
as speculation and precise state.

6
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:37,600
So with the scoreboard, false dependencies became hazards and we were unable to provide

7
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:40,040
support for misspeculations.

8
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:45,160
The underlying reason for this is the right backstage is actually a combination of two

9
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:47,040
fundamental operations.

10
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:52,520
One, we need to propagate a result to younger dependent instructions.

11
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:55,520
This is okay to be done out of order.

12
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:00,760
And two, we need to write a result into the actual register file.

13
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:06,960
This would be better to be done in order so that at any point in time, we have a proper

14
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:15,640
snapshot of the state of all registers as if no one had executed out of order.

15
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:21,000
So what the MIPS R10K processor does is replaces the scoreboard with the reorder buffer or

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,640
ROB.

17
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:27,320
Its goal is to buffer completed results en route to the register file so that no matter

18
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:32,120
what arbitrary order the instructions finish executing, we can push their results to the

19
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:34,360
register file in order.

20
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:38,640
With this, we break up the right backstage into two new stages.

21
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:43,760
Complete is an out-of-order stage where after executing the operation, the instruction then

22
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:50,160
writes its result to the ROB in order to pass the value to any dependents.

23
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:54,800
Then retire is actually an in-order stage that comes after completion.

24
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:59,760
And its goal is to allow instructions to commit their changes into the register file and leave

25
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:03,320
the pipeline in the order that they were fetched.

26
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,160
Note that retire is sometimes also called commit or graduate.

27
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,080
This is a conceptual idea.

28
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:15,000
We'll get into the details of how to implement this.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,600
The MIPS R10K employs register renaming, which as discussed removes WAR and WAW hazards by

30
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:28,000
effectively having multiple physical versions of architectural registers.

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,480
This completely eliminates the architectural register file.

32
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:37,280
Instead, we use a physical register file, and the map table simply tracks which physical

33
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:43,960
register currently holds the most recent value of each architectural register.

34
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:47,760
The free list keeps track of all unallocated physical registers.

35
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:52,000
To avoid running out of physical registers, the physical register file should be as large

36
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,520
as the number of architectural registers in the ISA plus the number of ROB entries.

37
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:02,440
This ensures enough physical registers for the worst case scenario where every single

38
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:09,800
instruction in the ROB is modifying a register.

39
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:14,640
An important question is, when is it safe to put physical registers back into the free

40
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:15,640
list?

41
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:20,280
When an instruction retires, the currently mapped physical register can't be freed

42
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:23,720
because younger instructions may still need to read this value.

43
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:29,760
However, the previously mapped physical register can now be freed upon retirement.

44
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:31,280
Why is this?

45
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:35,480
Because all instructions that will ever read this previous value must have already been

46
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:43,200
retired by now, if it's our turn to retire and we're overriding this register.

47
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,480
Now we'll describe the operation of our MIPS R10K processor.

48
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:52,240
Note that this is simply one manifestation of the principles of the original MIPS R10K

49
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:53,400
work.

50
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:57,640
Different implementations and variations are certainly possible.

51
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:03,280
We'll discuss a simplified MIPS R10K-like processor for the purposes of this course.

52
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,080
We'll make the following assumptions.

53
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:10,840
We'll assume that the front end stages, that is fetch, decode, and dispatch, are scalar.

54
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:16,640
Modern systems are likely to be able to fetch and decode multiple instructions in parallel.

55
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:21,080
We'll also assume that branches are resolved at the execute stage, and each reservation

56
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:26,080
station, which is a buffer for instructions waiting to perform an operation, each one

57
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,080
is bound to a functional unit.

58
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:33,440
That is, if our pipeline has three load units, then we'll have three reservation stations,

59
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:39,240
each dedicated to one load unit.

60
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:43,360
Let's describe the necessary hardware structures for the R10K processor.

61
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:47,080
First, we introduce reservation stations.

62
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:51,760
As just discussed, this is a buffer for tracking which instructions are waiting to use a specific

63
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:56,680
functional unit and tracking which operand registers they're waiting for.

64
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:02,800
Then we also have the ROB, map table, and free list, as previously discussed.

65
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:07,960
Now we'll illustrate how each pipeline stage is performed using the example add instruction

66
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,560
shown.

67
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:14,800
The fetch and decode stages are effectively still the same.

68
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:19,360
We need to get the instruction from memory, update the PC, and decode the operation and

69
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:23,400
operands.

70
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:28,760
As with the scoreboard, the dispatch stage is in order and initializes important hardware

71
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:29,760
structures.

72
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:32,500
First, we need to check for structural hazards.

73
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:37,740
If either there's no available reservation station, the reorder buffer is full, or there's

74
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:45,100
no free physical register on the free list, then we need to stall.

75
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:50,220
Once these structures are made available, we need to allocate a reservation station,

76
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:54,540
consult the map table to get the physical registers of our operands, as well as the

77
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:59,020
ready bits and record them in the reservation station.

78
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:03,080
In if our instruction writes to a register, we allocate a new physical register from the

79
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:06,940
free list and update the map table, clearing the ready bit.

80
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:11,260
Finally, we allocate the next entry in the reorder buffer, recording the old and new

81
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:16,220
physical register mappings.

82
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:20,620
Like in scoreboard, the issue stage is where instructions wait for their true dependences

83
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:22,620
to be resolved.

84
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:28,140
The only difference is now the wait in a reservation station as opposed to in the scoreboard.

85
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:32,060
An instruction must also wait here if there are no available functional units.

86
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:36,420
This is only possible in implementations where the number of reservation stations exceeds

87
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:38,580
the number of functional units.

88
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:43,420
So in these cases, an instruction will have to wait until a functional unit frees up.

89
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:48,060
In our simple implementation, we just assume a 1 to 1 ratio of reservation stations to

90
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:50,340
functional units.

91
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:54,620
Once an instruction is ready to issue, it moves into the functional unit and frees up

92
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:58,500
its reservation station.

93
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:03,500
The execute stage is identical to before, though note that different operations may

94
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:08,780
take more cycles to execute than others.

95
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:11,780
Complete is one of the new stages in R10K.

96
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:14,620
Its goal is to resolve RAW dependences.

97
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:19,220
If an instruction produces an output, the result will be written to the allocated physical

98
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:20,220
register.

99
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:24,660
Then the map table and all reservation stations that are currently waiting for this register

100
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:29,100
will be notified that it's ready to be read.

101
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:34,380
So in this example, the add instruction writes its result into physical register P1, which

102
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:38,940
sets the ready bit in the map table as well as the ready bits of the dependent instructions

103
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:41,060
in the reservation stations.

104
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:48,100
In this figure, the ready bits are shown as plus signs.

105
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:49,900
The last stage is retire.

106
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:55,380
This is the only in-order stage in the back end, and since it's in order, only the oldest

107
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:59,540
unretired instruction is allowed to retire at any cycle.

108
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:04,540
So even if an instruction has completed every other stage, it cannot perform the retire

109
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:09,060
operations until it's at the head of the reorder buffer.

110
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:12,500
Once an instruction is finally at the head of the reorder buffer and has entered the

111
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:15,780
retire stage, it does two key things.

112
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:20,820
First, it frees the old physical register that was mapped to its destination architectural

113
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:22,260
register.

114
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:27,740
In this example, this is P5, which was recorded in the ROB entry.

115
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:33,340
As discussed previously, it is now safe to free P5 here because any instructions that

116
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:40,260
needed to read P5 must have now retired since our add instruction is next in line to retire.

117
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:45,060
The second thing we need to do is free up the ROB entry, which makes the next instruction

118
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:47,020
the head.

119
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:49,380
Two additional things happen in retire.

120
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:55,140
If we're a store instruction, now is actually when we perform the write to memory instead

121
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:57,180
of in the execute stage.

122
00:08:57,180 --> 00:09:03,080
The reason for this is to maintain precise state in the event of misspeculation or exceptions.

123
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:08,100
If we had performed the store operation during the execute stage, we risk modifying memory

124
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:12,260
and then later realizing that we need to flush the store.

125
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:17,460
Once a store instruction is ready to retire and is at the head of the ROB, only then can

126
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:22,740
we guarantee that it won't get flushed anymore so we can finally write to memory.

127
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:27,580
Any exceptions are also handled here so that we make sure the exceptions are always handled

128
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:33,300
in order.

129
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:38,540
Now let's see the MIPS R10K processor in action within an example program.

130
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:43,100
The top shows the pipeline diagram and the vertical line shows which point in time we're

131
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:44,100
currently in.

132
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:49,060
We'll go over how each of the hardware structures are updated at each cycle.

133
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:53,540
So at the end of cycle 2, the load instruction has been decoded and the branch instruction

134
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:55,180
has been fetched.

135
00:09:55,180 --> 00:10:04,340
These stages don't affect the structures we're showing here.

136
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:07,860
Moving on to the end of cycle 3, the load has been dispatched.

137
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:12,220
As shown, we allocate a reservation station for the load and record the physical register

138
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:14,060
of its operand.

139
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:20,060
The load only has one input operand, which is register S0, and according to the map table,

140
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:25,260
this is currently mapped to physical register P6, which is ready.

141
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:30,620
Since the load is writing to register T0, it needs to allocate a new physical register

142
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:32,060
from the free list.

143
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:39,020
So we pop P1 from the free list and record it in the map table as well as the ROB entry.

144
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:45,180
And since T0 was previously mapped to P5, we record P5 in the ROB entry as well.

145
00:10:45,180 --> 00:10:53,540
In the meantime, the branch is predicted to not be taken.

146
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:58,940
At the end of cycle 4, the load has issued since all its operands are ready.

147
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:02,340
Thus we remove it from the reservation station.

148
00:11:02,340 --> 00:11:05,300
At the same time, the branch instruction is dispatched.

149
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:10,700
Its two operands are found to be mapped P1 and P4, but P1 is currently not ready since

150
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:15,380
it's being produced by the load instruction, hence the minus sign.

151
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:20,420
The branch is allocated in the ROB, and this time no register renaming needs to take place

152
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:27,660
because the branch instruction doesn't modify any registers.

153
00:11:27,660 --> 00:11:31,500
Now in the end of cycle 5, the load instruction has started executing.

154
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:35,460
Here, we're assuming loads take three cycles to execute.

155
00:11:35,460 --> 00:11:40,700
The branch is currently in the issue stage, but since not all of its operands are ready,

156
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:44,140
it needs to stay in the issue stage for the next cycle.

157
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:49,060
In the meantime, the add-i instruction is able to dispatch following the same procedure

158
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:55,860
as the others.

159
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:57,900
In cycle 6, not much changes.

160
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:02,020
The load is still executing, thus the branch and add-i instructions that are dependent

161
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:04,980
on it must continue to wait in issue.

162
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:10,420
At the same time, the subtract instruction is able to dispatch.

163
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:19,340
Similarly, in cycle 7, the add-i and branch continue to wait for the load to finish executing.

164
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:24,220
The subtract instruction, however, is able to successfully issue since both of its operands

165
00:12:24,220 --> 00:12:25,420
are ready.

166
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:29,060
So then it frees up its reservation station.

167
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:33,820
Now the second add-i instruction is ready to dispatch, but it must actually stall because

168
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:35,740
of a structural hazard.

169
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:43,580
There are no free reservation stations for add-i available right now.

170
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:46,380
Now at the end of cycle 8, lots of things happen.

171
00:12:46,380 --> 00:12:51,340
Finally, the load instruction is completed, so it was written its value to the physical

172
00:12:51,340 --> 00:12:55,860
register file and notified all of its dependents that the data is ready.

173
00:12:55,860 --> 00:13:00,940
So the dependents, which are now the branch and add-i instructions, are now able to successfully

174
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:06,380
issue in this stage, thus removing themselves from their reservation stations.

175
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:10,740
And because the first add-i instruction is able to exit its reservation station this

176
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:15,740
cycle, the second add-i instruction, which was stalled last cycle, can now successfully

177
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:20,500
dispatch as shown here.

178
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:25,940
In cycle 9, the load instruction is able to retire, so it removes itself from the ROB

179
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:30,220
and pushes the original physical register P5 into the free list.

180
00:13:30,220 --> 00:13:36,380
Meanwhile, the subtract instruction completes, which passes its result to the second add-i

181
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:39,380
instruction, which is now able to issue.

182
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:44,300
And since the second add-i instruction issues and exits the reservation station, the third

183
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:53,340
add-i instruction can now be dispatched at the same time.

184
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:58,100
Now in cycle 10, both the branch and first add-i instructions complete.

185
00:13:58,100 --> 00:14:02,380
The branch does nothing in the complete stage, while the add-i writes its result into the

186
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:07,540
physical register file and sets its ready bit in the map table as shown.

187
00:14:07,540 --> 00:14:12,820
The subtract, which executed earlier, is now ready to retire, but it can't, since it's

188
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:15,220
not at the head of the ROB.

189
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:19,580
And meanwhile, the third add-i instruction successfully issues and leaves the reservation

190
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:26,380
station.

191
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:31,340
In cycle 11, three instructions are ready to retire, but only one instruction can be retired

192
00:14:31,340 --> 00:14:34,860
in the cycle, and it has to be in order.

193
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:40,100
So only the branch instruction is able to retire, removing itself from the ROB.

194
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:44,380
Note that the second add-i instruction completes in this cycle, but it doesn't modify the

195
00:14:44,380 --> 00:14:48,140
ready bit for register T0 in the map table.

196
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:53,300
That's because it no longer holds the most up-to-date value of register T0.

197
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:58,020
It can tell because its result went to physical register 8, but the map table entry is now

198
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:04,340
pointing to something else, P7, which implies that another instruction after is modifying

199
00:15:04,340 --> 00:15:16,660
register T0, and that instruction is not ready, so the bit should remain cleared.

200
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:21,900
Now at the end of cycle 12, the first add-i instruction is able to retire and push P9 back

201
00:15:21,900 --> 00:15:23,260
into the free list.

202
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:29,220
Meanwhile, the last add-i instruction is completed, and since it is the most recent version of

203
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:37,540
register T0, it sets the ready bit in the map table.

204
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:41,820
This process continues until the end of the program, and the final pipeline diagram is

205
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:50,460
shown above.

206
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:55,020
One of the big components of the MIPS R10K processor is its support for speculation and

207
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:56,660
precise state.

208
00:15:56,660 --> 00:16:02,060
So how do we properly flush the pipeline in the event of mis-speculation or exception?

209
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:06,700
Like scoreboard, physical registers are written out of order in the complete stage.

210
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:11,340
The main difference is that unlike in scoreboard, the ROB and map table preserve the original

211
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:15,940
mappings of physical registers, just in case we end up needing to flush this instruction

212
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:17,780
later.

213
00:16:17,780 --> 00:16:22,180
As long as we always retire instructions in order, we will always be able to revert any

214
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:28,100
changes we've made to registers or to memory in the event of mis-speculation.

215
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:32,900
To do this, we simply need to walk backwards through the ROB and roll back the contents

216
00:16:32,900 --> 00:16:36,700
of the map table and the free list one by one.

217
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:39,900
Note that there's no need to modify the physical register file.

218
00:16:39,900 --> 00:16:41,140
The data can stay there.

219
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:47,820
We only need to put physical registers back on the free list.

220
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:52,020
Let's walk through the process of handling mis-speculation using the example program

221
00:16:52,020 --> 00:16:55,700
from earlier.

222
00:16:55,700 --> 00:17:00,140
So earlier, we predicted the branch to be not taken, but what if after resolving it in

223
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:03,180
the execute stage, we find that it's actually taken?

224
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:12,220
How do we flush all of these instructions?

225
00:17:12,220 --> 00:17:14,060
It's actually quite straightforward.

226
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:17,540
We just start from the youngest instruction and work backwards.

227
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:21,260
So in this case, the third add-i instruction is the youngest.

228
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:25,740
So we've removed it from the reservation station and the ROB.

229
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:31,740
Then we put its physical register P7 back into the free list and revert P8 back into

230
00:17:31,740 --> 00:17:35,540
the map table.

231
00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:38,940
Then we move on to the next youngest instruction and do the same thing.

232
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:45,860
We clear it from the ROB and revert the map table and the free list.

233
00:17:45,860 --> 00:17:50,300
This process simply continues until we've cleared all ROB entries that came after the

234
00:17:50,300 --> 00:17:52,620
branch instruction.

235
00:17:52,620 --> 00:17:56,340
Then at this point, the branch is able to complete and we immediately start fetching

236
00:17:56,340 --> 00:18:02,860
from the taken target PC.

237
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:05,140
So let's reiterate some key takeaways.

238
00:18:05,140 --> 00:18:12,060
Fetch, decode, dispatch, and retire are all in order stages, while issue, execute, and

239
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:14,980
complete are out of order.

240
00:18:14,980 --> 00:18:19,380
The reason why this scheme is able to maintain precise state is because all changes to register

241
00:18:19,380 --> 00:18:25,380
mappings happen in order, either via the dispatch stage or the retire stage.

242
00:18:25,380 --> 00:18:29,540
And one last reminder of the distinction between stalling, where all younger instructions

243
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:38,220
must stall too, versus waiting, where younger instructions may bypass out of order.

244
00:18:38,220 --> 00:18:43,860
To sum up, we covered two fundamental implementations of superscalar processors, scoreboard and

245
00:18:43,860 --> 00:18:44,860
R10K.

246
00:18:44,860 --> 00:18:51,060
An analogy of their key concepts is say multiple students are working on different projects

247
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:54,260
in the same computing cluster.

248
00:18:54,260 --> 00:18:59,980
With scoreboard, these projects can be executed in any arbitrary out of order, that is, some

249
00:18:59,980 --> 00:19:03,420
students who started later can still end up finishing first.

250
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:08,740
However, there's only one installation of LLVM allowed in the system at any moment.

251
00:19:08,740 --> 00:19:13,020
So even though all projects are independent from each other, they may need to wait for

252
00:19:13,020 --> 00:19:18,460
each other to finish, since they may need different versions of LLVM.

253
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:23,780
But with R10K, we break this hazard by installing the different versions of LLVM in temporary

254
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:25,360
directories.

255
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:29,940
We maintain precise state by using a main symbolic link that points to the most recent

256
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:30,940
version.

257
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:35,700
But projects can now run fully out of order without waiting for each other, because they

258
00:19:35,700 --> 00:19:44,740
can use temporary copies of LLVM if need be.

259
00:19:44,740 --> 00:19:48,060
So let's close out this lecture with a quick exercise.

260
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:51,980
The top shows an example program that has been renamed, with the contents of the map

261
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:54,660
table and free list shown over time.

262
00:19:54,660 --> 00:20:00,540
The question is which physical registers are freed when each of the instructions are retired?

263
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:10,060
Take some time now to try to fill in the blanks.

264
00:20:10,060 --> 00:20:14,820
So when we dispatch the add instruction, we renamed its output to P4, but when the add

265
00:20:14,820 --> 00:20:19,940
retires, P4 does not go back into the free list, but rather, the original mapping of

266
00:20:19,940 --> 00:20:28,300
register T1 is what goes into the free list, which is physical register P1.

267
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:33,460
So similarly, when the NOR instruction retires, the original mapping for architectural register

268
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:37,780
T3 is freed, which is P3.

269
00:20:37,780 --> 00:20:43,660
Then the subtract overrides T3 again, and it ends up freeing P5, which is the physical

270
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:48,420
register that was just allocated to the NOR instruction.

271
00:20:48,420 --> 00:20:54,140
And finally, the ORI instruction frees up P4, which is the physical register that was

272
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:56,460
allocated to the add instruction earlier.

